          "question": "For what value of $k$ is the function $f(x) = \\begin{cases} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{4 + x} - 2}{x}, & x \\ne 0 \\\\ k, & x = 0 \\end{cases}$ continuous at $x = 0$?",
          "options": [
            "$0$",
            "$\\dfrac{1}{4}$",
            "$1$",
            "$4$"
          ],
          "solution": {
            "answer": "(B) $\\dfrac{1}{4}$",
            "concept": "Continuous at $0$ means $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x \\to 0} f(x) = f(0)$; a $\\dfrac{0}{0}$ root form is rationalised.",
            "steps": [
              {
                "do": "Rationalise the numerator.",
                "math": "\\dfrac{\\sqrt{4+x} - 2}{x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{4+x} + 2}{\\sqrt{4+x} + 2} = \\dfrac{(4 + x) - 4}{x\\left(\\sqrt{4+x} + 2\\right)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{4+x} + 2}"
              },
              {
                "do": "Take the limit as $x \\to 0$ and equate to $f(0) = k$.",
                "math": "\\lim_{x \\to 0}\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{4+x} + 2} = \\dfrac{1}{2 + 2} = \\dfrac{1}{4} = k",
                "marks": 1
              },
              {
                "do": "So the correct option is (B)."
              }
            ],
            "mistake": "Putting $x = 0$ straight in gives $\\dfrac{0}{0}$; rationalise first, do not guess $k = 0$."
          }
        },
